// @test: parse-pass
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: meta, meta-functions, macros

// A `meta <name>(...)` declaration makes `@name(...)` a DECLARED macro, not
// an unknown meta-function — and the declaration may come AFTER the call,
// which a single-pass parser cannot know at the call site.  The filter
// therefore runs once per parse, with the whole item list in hand.
//
// Without it, every file declaring its own macros reported them as unknown:
// the warning named the very declaration three lines up.
//
// Expansion itself is a separate matter.  Calling one is refused by the
// type checker with a diagnostic that says which of the two problems it is,
// rather than the `nil` the call used to produce.

fn main() -> Int {
    @twice(21);
    return 0;
}

meta twice(x: expr) {
    x => x + x
}
